/-
# AC-UNIFICATION is NP-complete

Source:
D. Kapur and P. Narendran, "Complexity of unification problems with associative-commutative
operators", Journal of Automated Reasoning 9 (1992), 261-288, doi:10.1007/BF00245463
(membership in NP, hence NP-completeness); D. Benanav, D. Kapur and P. Narendran, "Complexity of
matching problems", Journal of Symbolic Computation 3 (1987), 203-216,
doi:10.1016/S0747-7171(87)80027-5 (NP-hardness). Both results, and the definition used here,
are recorded in D. Kapur and P. Narendran, "Double-exponential complexity of computing a
complete set of AC-unifiers", Proc. 7th IEEE Symposium on Logic in Computer Science (LICS 1992),
11-21, doi:10.1109/LICS.1992.185515, Section 1.
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Defs
public import CSLibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.ACUnificationDefs

@[expose] public section

namespace MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.ACUnification

open FirstOrder FirstOrder.Language
open MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.UnificationForFinitelyPresentedAlgebras (lang tokens)
open MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.ACMatching (ACEquiv)

/-!
# AC-UNIFICATION is NP-complete

INSTANCE: a finite set of function symbols with arities, some of the binary ones declared
associative-commutative (AC), and two first-order terms `s` and `t` over these symbols and a
finite set `V` of variables. QUESTION: is there a substitution `σ` such that
`σ(s) =_AC σ(t)`, where `=_AC` stands for the equivalence relation on terms generated by the
associativity and commutativity of the AC function symbols (Kapur and Narendran, LICS 1992,
Section 1)?

The symbols, the terms, their token-list encoding and `=_AC` (`ACEquiv`) are those of
`ACMatchingWanted`; here both terms may contain variables. A substitution maps each variable of
`V` to a term over the countably many variables `ℕ` (`Term.subst`), so the range of `σ` may
contain variables.

Encoding: the instance is decoded from a bitstring with the repository's
canonical `MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding`. Undecodable bitstrings, and
bitstrings decoding to malformed instances, are no-instances.

NP-completeness is stated in the same form as the proved results in
`CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Karp`: membership in
`MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP`, plus a polynomial-time many-one
reduction from every language in `NP`
(`Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed`).
-/

/-- **AC-UNIFICATION is NP-complete.** Source: Kapur and Narendran (1992); Benanav, Kapur and
Narendran (1987). -/
public theorem_wanted acUnification_npComplete :
    acUnificationDec ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP ∧
      ∀ L : MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem,
        L ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP →
          Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed L acUnificationDec

end MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.ACUnification
